// Package modding loads and runs sandboxed TSX/JS game mods on top of NSGo's tsx runtime.
//
// A mod lives in a directory containing a mod.yaml manifest ([ModManifest], version 1 or 2, read by [ParseManifest] and written by [EncodeManifest]) and an entry script (such as mod.tsx or mod.js), or content directories declared under contributes for content-only version 2 mods. Mods import the capability-gated "game" module to subscribe to simulation steps and events, emit events, spawn entities, read and write scoped game state, play audio, persist per-mod storage, write logs, and optionally mount TSX UI panels or HUD widgets. Hosts can also register custom TSX modules ([ModModule]) gated by host-defined dotted capabilities ([HostCapability]), look up the calling mod with [Caller], enforce per-call permissions with [ModAPI.RequireHost], narrow grants with [ManagerOptions.GrantHost] and [Manager.SetHostGrant], and emit TypeScript declarations with [WriteModAPIDTS].
//
// [Manager] discovers mods from the filesystem or any [io/fs.FS], isolates discovery failures per mod ([StateFailed] on [ModInfo]) so one broken mod never blocks sibling mods, installs or removes individual mods dynamically ([Manager.Install], [Manager.Uninstall]), validates manifests and platform filters, resolves required dependencies, optional dependencies, and conflicts ([ErrConflictingMods]) in deterministic topological order, overlays loaded mods' contributed content directories through [Manager.ContentFS] with strict mod-directory path and symlink confinement, isolates runtime faults, persists enabled/disabled state via [core.Storage], and drives step hooks from [game.Loop].
//
// [Workshop] integrates Steam Workshop (Steamworks ISteamUGC) with [Manager] through a [WorkshopBackend]: [NewSteamWorkshop] binds the Steamworks flat C API via purego on macOS, Linux and Windows (returning [ErrWorkshopUnavailable] when Steam is absent or on web/mobile), while [NewMemoryWorkshop] simulates multi-user publishing, subscriptions and updates on disk for tests and offline workflows. Published mods store deterministic Workshop metadata in workshop.yaml ([WorkshopMetadata]).
package modding
